WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00.390 --> 00:05.603
When we think about facilitating a meeting, or indeed facilitating a conversation,

2
00:05.603 --> 00:09.990
one helpful framework to use is open, narrow, close. So what is that?

3
00:11.140 --> 00:15.230
These are the three main phases you will have in an interaction.

4
00:16.520 --> 00:19.230
Opening is exactly what it sounds like.

5
00:20.010 --> 00:25.190
It’s opening up the issues or the ideas; it’s starting the meeting, kicking it off,

6
00:27.110 --> 00:31.390
and broadening the perspective of what you’re talking about. Narrowing

7
00:32.360 --> 00:35.490
is, once you’ve opened everything up, you need to focus,

8
00:36.010 --> 00:41.350
maybe prioritise, work out really what you want to solve for in that meeting.

9
00:42.850 --> 00:48.960
And finally, closing — it’s not rocket science — is bringing it all to a successful conclusion:

10
00:49.610 --> 00:52.160
thinking about next steps, commitments,

11
00:52.610 --> 00:58.660
making sure everybody agrees what they’ve agreed, making sure you get takeaways, gather takeaways,

12
00:59.240 --> 01:03.330
so that people move on from the meeting in the most effective way possible.

13
01:04.330 --> 01:12.100
Open, narrow and close. How do you decide when to move from open to narrowing and then closing?

14
01:13.800 --> 01:21.090
That depends on several things. It depends on whether this is the last meeting you’ll ever have on the subject, or an initial meeting.

15
01:21.730 --> 01:29.760
It also depends on how good the ideas need to be. So when you’re planning your workshop, you’ll need to decide how much time

16
01:30.090 --> 01:34.340
to give to each stage of open, narrow and close.

17
01:35.080 --> 01:39.840
This takes some experience to get right, but there are some general guidelines.

18
01:41.220 --> 01:48.100
The opening section, in terms of introduction and sharing the agenda, you want to move through fairly quickly.

19
01:48.450 --> 01:53.236
People can get frustrated if they don’t get onto the real content quite quickly.

20
01:53.236 --> 01:58.500
Once you go onto the second part of opening, which is really about opening up the ideas,

21
01:59.050 --> 02:05.060
you might want to give that more time. At the same time, it’s really about creativity and dynamism.

22
02:05.770 --> 02:08.320
So you don’t want a twenty-minute brainstorm.

23
02:08.930 --> 02:19.070
You’ll actually get much better results if you say, “Okay, we’re going to get as many ideas on the table as we can in three minutes. Let’s start the stop clock now.”

24
02:19.970 --> 02:24.470
People’s energy will be much higher and they will fire those ideas out.

25
02:24.470 --> 02:29.730
So open, I think, you want to move through; you want to create energy and momentum.

26
02:30.250 --> 02:38.258
For the narrow phase, that often takes more time and actually more facilitative skill, because really what you’re doing is making hard decisions.

27
02:38.258 --> 02:45.880
Let’s say you’ve got thirty initiatives on the table and everyone around the room has their own favourites, so there’s much more jeopardy,

28
02:46.280 --> 02:49.890
and people’s egos are on the line when they’ve offered ideas already.

29
02:50.660 --> 02:55.073
Do allow good time for that narrowing phase. People need to be on board,

30
02:55.073 --> 03:01.570
and they need to believe in the fairness of the process that got to the conclusions you get. With closing,

31
03:02.150 --> 03:07.750
I think you want to again go into momentum mode, because you’re looking for commitments.

32
03:08.360 --> 03:11.430
So you don’t want to get too stuck down.

33
03:12.330 --> 03:22.900
Hopefully you’ve actually had some conclusions through the meeting, so it might be partly about restating what has been agreed explicitly at the end, so that everyone is on board.

34
03:22.900 --> 03:26.620
So I would allow maybe twenty minutes to half an hour for that,

35
03:26.760 --> 03:36.770
even in a day-long meeting. So those are some of the general guidelines about open, narrow, close and how to portion time throughout the meeting.

36
03:37.410 --> 03:45.470
Of course, it still depends on context, and understanding what is most important for you to achieve during that interaction.

